WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.215
The café's payments program takes a lock every time it changes a balance.

2
00:00:07.215 --> 00:00:12.719
Picture one thread doing that a million times, while no other thread ever wants the lock.

3
00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:18.154
Only the kernel, the core of the operating system, can put a thread to sleep or wake it.

4
00:00:18.154 --> 00:00:21.916
A request from a program to the kernel is called a system call.

5
00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:24.880
So how many system calls does this loop make?

6
00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:26.880
Make a guess, and hold on to it.

7
00:00:27.467 --> 00:00:31.835
The lock is one number in the program's memory, the lock word.

8
00:00:31.835 --> 00:00:33.667
0 is free, and 1 is taken.

9
00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:37.796
T1, a refund, and T2, a payment, both want it.

10
00:00:37.796 --> 00:00:46.192
A thread takes it with compare and swap, one instruction: if this holds 0, put 1 in it, and tell me whether you did.

11
00:00:46.333 --> 00:00:48.537
T1 goes first, and gets it.

12
00:00:48.733 --> 00:00:50.310
T2 finds 1, and fails.

13
00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:56.256
Trying again in a loop would burn its turn on the CPU, while T1 isn't even running.

14
00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.756
It should sleep instead, and only the kernel can do that.

15
00:00:59.933 --> 00:01:06.063
The obvious design asks the kernel every time: once to take the lock, and once to release it.

16
00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:09.413
A system call costs about 150 nanoseconds.

17
00:01:09.413 --> 00:01:15.481
A million free locks, taken and released inside the program, take 8 milliseconds.

18
00:01:15.481 --> 00:01:18.703
Two system calls a pair add about 300 more.

19
00:01:18.867 --> 00:01:22.281
Nearly 40 times slower, for a wait that never happens.

20
00:01:22.281 --> 00:01:26.517
It's like asking the barista's permission to use an empty bathroom.

21
00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:31.315
Wouldn't it be nice if the kernel stayed out of it, until someone really had to wait?

22
00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:37.677
In a café, you see Vacant on the bathroom door, go in and lock it, and later you come out.

23
00:01:37.677 --> 00:01:38.917
Nobody asks anyone.

24
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:45.300
Only when the door is already locked do you go to the counter, and ask the barista to call you when it's free.

25
00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:49.801
And you leave a note on the door, so whoever comes out knows someone is waiting.

26
00:01:49.933 --> 00:01:50.820
Let's map it.

27
00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:55.049
The door's sign is the lock word, in the program's own memory.

28
00:01:55.049 --> 00:01:56.823
Each customer is a thread.

29
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.913
The barista is the kernel, and the list of names at the counter is its wait queue.

30
00:02:02.067 --> 00:02:04.096
And the note is a third value.

31
00:02:04.096 --> 00:02:06.667
2 means taken, and someone is waiting.

32
00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:09.809
Now one round, counting system calls.

33
00:02:09.809 --> 00:02:11.127
T1 wants the lock.

34
00:02:11.127 --> 00:02:14.788
The word is 0, so one compare and swap makes it 1.

35
00:02:14.788 --> 00:02:18.156
T1 is in, and the kernel never heard about it.

36
00:02:18.267 --> 00:02:22.519
T2's compare and swap expects 0, finds 1, and fails.

37
00:02:22.667 --> 00:02:28.170
So T2 sets the word to 2, the note, and makes the first system call: futex wait.

38
00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:31.747
Put me to sleep, but only if the word still holds 2.

39
00:02:31.867 --> 00:02:35.665
The kernel checks, and puts T2 on the wait queue.

40
00:02:35.665 --> 00:02:38.301
T2 sleeps, and uses no CPU at all.

41
00:02:38.467 --> 00:02:39.353
T1 finishes.

42
00:02:39.353 --> 00:02:43.044
It sets the word to 0, and looks at the old value.

43
00:02:43.044 --> 00:02:48.065
It was 2, so T1 makes the second call: futex wake, wake one sleeper.

44
00:02:48.167 --> 00:02:50.507
The kernel takes T2 off the queue.

45
00:02:50.507 --> 00:02:52.778
T2 tries again and gets the lock.

46
00:02:52.778 --> 00:02:56.357
It writes 2, to be safe: others may still be asleep.

47
00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:58.446
One wait cost two system calls.

48
00:02:58.446 --> 00:03:01.271
With nobody waiting, it would have cost none.

49
00:03:01.633 --> 00:03:05.510
This is called a futex, short for fast user space mutex.

50
00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:07.794
Mutex is another name for a lock.

51
00:03:07.794 --> 00:03:12.085
User space is where ordinary programs run, outside the kernel.

52
00:03:12.233 --> 00:03:15.207
Why does futex wait check the word before it sleeps?

53
00:03:15.207 --> 00:03:17.495
Let's be fair to a version that doesn't.

54
00:03:17.667 --> 00:03:21.047
T1 holds the lock, and T2 sets the word to 2.

55
00:03:21.047 --> 00:03:25.028
But before T2's call reaches the kernel, T1 finishes.

56
00:03:25.028 --> 00:03:28.183
It sets 0, sees the old 2, and calls wake.

57
00:03:28.183 --> 00:03:31.112
The queue is empty, so nobody wakes up.

58
00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.093
Now T2's call arrives.

59
00:03:33.093 --> 00:03:34.728
What happens to T2?

60
00:03:34.728 --> 00:03:36.105
Pause and guess.

61
00:03:39.333 --> 00:03:42.458
T2 falls asleep, and nobody will ever wake it.

62
00:03:42.458 --> 00:03:46.941
The lock is free, and T2 waits outside an empty bathroom, forever.

63
00:03:46.941 --> 00:03:48.911
That's called a lost wake-up.

64
00:03:49.067 --> 00:03:55.413
With the check, the kernel sees 0 instead of the 2 that T2 expected, and won't put it to sleep.

65
00:03:55.413 --> 00:04:01.091
The call fails at once with an error called EAGAIN, try again, and T2 takes the lock.

66
00:04:01.233 --> 00:04:05.912
It's the barista glancing at the door once more, before writing your name down.

67
00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:08.459
Now, your guess from the start.

68
00:04:08.459 --> 00:04:12.643
strace is a tool that counts every system call a program makes.

69
00:04:12.833 --> 00:04:16.717
One thread, a million locks nobody else wanted: 1 futex call.

70
00:04:16.717 --> 00:04:22.383
And that one isn't even the lock: it's the main thread, waiting for the worker to finish.

71
00:04:22.533 --> 00:04:26.914
Four threads, all fighting over one lock: 2,749 calls.

72
00:04:26.914 --> 00:04:33.972
And 1,042 of them failed with EAGAIN: the check, catching the same race, over and over.

73
00:04:33.972 --> 00:04:37.785
A lock is only free while nobody else wants it.

74
00:04:38.133 --> 00:04:40.758
Where the café picture breaks: fairness.

75
00:04:40.758 --> 00:04:42.793
A barista calls names in order.

76
00:04:42.793 --> 00:04:50.143
A woken thread has to race any newcomer for the compare and swap, and the newcomer, already running, often wins.

77
00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:51.822
The kernel wakes you up.

78
00:04:51.822 --> 00:04:53.535
It doesn't save your place.

79
00:04:53.933 --> 00:04:58.591
So the moral of the story: ask the barista only when the door is locked.

80
00:04:58.591 --> 00:05:03.184
A free lock costs one instruction, and only waiting reaches the kernel.

81
00:05:03.533 --> 00:05:04.812
One quick check.

82
00:05:04.812 --> 00:05:09.047
T1 releases the lock, and the old value was 1, not 2.

83
00:05:09.047 --> 00:05:10.964
Does T1 call the kernel?

84
00:05:10.964 --> 00:05:12.243
Pause and think.

85
00:05:15.467 --> 00:05:15.691
No.

86
00:05:15.691 --> 00:05:19.429
1 means no note on the door, so nobody is waiting.

87
00:05:19.429 --> 00:05:22.271
Releasing the lock is one instruction.

88
00:05:22.633 --> 00:05:27.444
And when waiting is this cheap, a thread that waits forever costs nothing either.

89
00:05:27.444 --> 00:05:30.711
That's how the café's card reader froze, and it's next.
